So, you decided that you wanted to make one too, first of all, good luck!

As for what makes this work, you need some software and hardware. 

First you will need the FreeVR app. 
This serves as the bridge between the display. It works by taking a screenshot of the currecnt screen and aplting lens corrections and splits the screen.
You can use command Q to quit tthe app once it is running. It will need permissions to record screen in privacy and security settings.
About it, it is basicaly fully vibe coded. I know, I know. 
But with out AI I would not have been able to complete the project before I would lose intrest. in my head, a nessisary evil.
I have tested it, and it works fairly well for me.

Once it is running, move it to the VR headset display and put it to fullscreen.

Next thing you need is the VR Track code. 
What it does is take the serial data coming from the ardrino and IMU. It may still require you to allow camera permissions
It does this because at first I intended that it would use april tag detection to correct for drift, but after I found there was minimal drift, I cut it to reduce overhead. 
The code is still there mostly. just commented out. to work the program, connect your ardrino via usb cable, then boot the program up.
then select the dropdown and select the port that apears. Be sure to click it then hit connect. It should then show the IMU quaterion on the debug window.
Next before xplane, you need to hit the button that says calabrate. follow the process and when it is done hit return to debug.
Once you have calabrated, it should, in theory be working.
You can quit the program using the escape key.

Ardrino
I used a generic nano and a BNO085 IMU Module. I used this one.
