The wiring is quite simple it uses the I2C protocal to work. 

NANO -> IMU
D9 ->   RST
D8 ->   INT
A4 ->   SDA
A5 ->   SCL
3.3v -> VCC
GND ->  GND

I put my components on a proto board.
https://www.amazon.com/Prototype-Snappable-Arduino-Electronics-Gold-Plated/dp/B08151V9TS?ref_=ast_sto_dp&th=1

XPLANE
I used X-Plane 12 as my simulator of choice. I do not know if 11 will work.
To set it up, you must go into network settings and hit the check mark that says use UNPnP for port forwarding, You must allow that.
It should now talk nicely to the VR Track code.

That should be about all on how this thing works. I'm sure I missed something, but comment any questions you have. I will get them and try to clarify the issue.

It should be noted that this is not going to be the best headset on the market, in fact it will most liekly be nowhere close. 
The intent was to show it is possible, and possible it is.

Thanks for finding this and I hope you can enjoy whatever you decide to do with it. 
